\title{Counting permutations of a given cycle type}
\taxon{Remark}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{The number of elements in #{S_n} with #{k_l} cycles of length #{l} is given by}

##{\frac{n!}{\prod_l (k_l!\,l^{k_l})}}

\p{(#{l} ranges over the different values in the cycle type; e.g. with cycle type #{(3, 2)}, #{k_3 = 1}, or #{(3, 1, 1)}, #{k_1 = 2}). Think of cycles as trays, and put in the elements of #{X = \lbrace 1, 2, \ldots, n\rbrace }: this gives #{n!} options. Each cycle of length #{l} can be written in #{l} ways; this gives the #{l^{k_l}} factor. Also #{k_l} cycles of length #{l} can be permuted in #{k_l!} ways.}

\p{E.g. the number of permutations in #{S_5} of type #{(2, 2, 1)}: #{k_2 = 2}, #{k_1 = 1}, so}

##{\# = \frac{5!}{(2!\,2^2)(1!\,1^1)} = 15.}

\p{Or #{(3, 2)}: #{k_3 = 1}, #{k_2 = 1}, so #{\# = \frac{5!}{(1!\,3^1)(1!\,2^1)} = 20}.}
